var e={level:`B1`,type:`multiple-choice`,questions:[{id:`b1-mc-001`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`I have lived in Rome ___ 2015.`,explanation:`Use 'since' with a point in time (2015) and 'for' with a period of time (ten years).`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for' is used with a length of time, such as 'for ten years'.`,2:`'from' needs 'to' to show a period.`,3:`'during' is followed by an event or period, not a starting year.`},options:[`for`,`since`,`from`,`during`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-002`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If it rains tomorrow, we ___ at home.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base verb.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The result clause needs 'will'.`,2:`'would stay' is for imaginary situations (second conditional).`,3:`The past simple does not fit with a future situation.`},options:[`stay`,`will stay`,`would stay`,`stayed`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-003`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Have you ever ___ sushi?`,explanation:`Present perfect: have/has + past participle. Example: Have you ever eaten Thai food?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'ate' is the past simple; after 'have' you need the past participle.`,1:`'eat' is the base form; the present perfect needs the past participle.`,2:`'eating' is the -ing form; it does not follow 'have' in the present perfect.`},options:[`ate`,`eat`,`eating`,`eaten`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-004`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`I ___ dinner when the phone rang.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for a longer action interrupted by a short one (past simple). Example: I was reading when she arrived.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'cooked' suggests you started cooking after the phone rang.`,2:`The present continuous does not fit with the past 'rang'.`,3:`The present perfect does not describe an action in progress in the past.`},options:[`was cooking`,`cooked`,`am cooking`,`have cooked`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-005`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If you heat ice, it ___.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for facts: if + present simple, present simple. Example: If you drop glass, it breaks.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not fit a general fact with 'heat'.`,2:`'melting' is not a full verb; the clause needs a present simple verb.`,3:`The present perfect is not used for general facts in the zero conditional.`},options:[`melted`,`melts`,`melting`,`has melted`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-006`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This bridge ___ in 1890.`,explanation:`Past passive: was/were + past participle. Example: The church was built in 1700.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A bridge cannot build; you need the passive 'was built'.`,1:`'in 1890' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'in 1890'.`},options:[`built`,`is built`,`was built`,`has built`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-007`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ wear a seatbelt in a car. It's the law.`,explanation:`Use 'have to' for rules and obligations. Example: You have to show your passport.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'might' shows possibility, not a legal rule.`,2:`'don't have to' means it isn't necessary, but the law says it is.`,3:`'could' shows possibility or ability, not obligation.`},options:[`have to`,`might`,`don't have to`,`could`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-008`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`When I was a child, I ___ play in the street every day.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base verb for past habits that are finished. Example: I used to walk to school.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,1:`'was used to' means 'was familiar with' and is followed by -ing or a noun.`,3:`'am used to' is present and means 'am familiar with'.`},options:[`use to`,`was used to`,`used to`,`am used to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-009`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`My brother hasn't finished his homework ___.`,explanation:`Use 'yet' at the end of negative sentences and questions. Example: She hasn't called yet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'already' is used in positive sentences, not with 'hasn't'.`,1:`'still' goes before the negative verb: 'He still hasn't finished'.`,2:`'ever' is used in questions and before the participle, not at the end.`},options:[`already`,`still`,`ever`,`yet`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-010`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`While we were walking in the park, it ___ to rain.`,explanation:`The short action that interrupts a longer one is in the past simple. Example: While I was sleeping, the alarm rang.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not fit in a story about the past.`,2:`The present perfect is not used for a short action at a past moment.`,3:`'start' is the base form and has no past meaning.`},options:[`starts`,`started`,`has started`,`start`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-011`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If I see Maria, I ___ her your message.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base form. Example: If I see him, I'll tell him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous is not used in the result of a first conditional.`,1:`'would give' is for imaginary situations, not a real future possibility.`,3:`The past simple does not talk about the future.`},options:[`am giving`,`would give`,`will give`,`gave`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-012`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`English ___ in many countries around the world.`,explanation:`Present passive: am/is/are + past participle. Example: Spanish is spoken in Mexico.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A language cannot speak; you need the passive.`,1:`This is active continuous; a language does not speak.`,3:`The passive needs the verb 'be': 'is spoken'.`},options:[`speaks`,`is speaking`,`is spoken`,`spoken`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-013`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You look tired. You ___ go to bed earlier.`,explanation:`Use 'should' to give advice. Example: You should drink more water.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't' means it is not allowed, which is not good advice here.`,1:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary, which is not advice.`,2:`'can't' means it is impossible or not allowed, not advice.`},options:[`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`can't`,`should`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-014`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Did you ___ have long hair?`,explanation:`In questions with 'did', use 'use to' (no -d). Example: Did she use to live here?`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'did', write 'use to' without -d.`,1:`'using to' is not a correct form.`,3:`'uses to' is not a correct form; 'used to' has no present.`},options:[`used to`,`using to`,`use to`,`uses to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-015`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`We have known each other ___ ten years.`,explanation:`Use 'for' with a length of time. Example: I have worked here for six months.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'since' needs a starting point, like 'since 2016', not a length of time.`,2:`'ago' is used with the past simple: 'ten years ago'.`,3:`'during' is followed by an event, like 'during the war', not by a length of time.`},options:[`for`,`since`,`ago`,`during`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-016`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`What ___ you doing at 8 o'clock last night?`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a time in the past. Example: What were you doing at noon?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'did' is followed by the base form, not by 'doing'.`,2:`'are' is present, but the question is about last night.`,3:`'was' is used with I/he/she/it, not with 'you'.`},options:[`were`,`did`,`are`,`was`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-017`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`We'll miss the bus if we ___ now.`,explanation:`In the if-clause, use the present simple, even for the future. Example: If you don't hurry, you'll be late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'if' in a first conditional, use the present simple, not 'will'.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'we'll miss'.`,3:`A negative present verb needs 'don't'.`},options:[`won't leave`,`didn't leave`,`don't leave`,`not leave`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-018`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The thieves ___ by the police last night.`,explanation:`Past passive with a plural subject: were + past participle. Example: The keys were found yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without 'were', the thieves did the catching, which makes no sense with 'by the police'.`,1:`'last night' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`'thieves' is plural, so you need 'were'.`},options:[`caught`,`are caught`,`were caught`,`was caught`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-019`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Take an umbrella. It ___ rain later.`,explanation:`Use 'might' for something that is possible in the future. Example: It might be cold tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has to' shows obligation, not possibility.`,1:`'can't' means it is impossible, so you wouldn't need an umbrella.`,3:`'mustn't' means something is not allowed; weather has no rules.`},options:[`has to`,`can't`,`might`,`mustn't`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-020`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`My dad ___ smoke, but he stopped ten years ago.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for a habit in the past that has stopped. Example: He used to drink a lot of coffee.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'used to' has no present form like 'uses to'.`,1:`'is used to' means 'is familiar with' and is followed by -ing.`,3:`'was using to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`uses to`,`is used to`,`used to`,`was using to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-021`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`I ___ this film three times, so I know the ending.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect for experiences with a number of times. Example: She has read that book twice.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'see' meaning watch with your eyes is not normally used in the continuous form here.`,1:`The present simple does not talk about experiences up to now.`,2:`The past continuous describes an action in progress, not a number of times.`},options:[`am seeing`,`see`,`was seeing`,`have seen`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-022`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`The children were playing outside ___ their mother called them in.`,explanation:`Use 'when' before a short action that interrupts a longer one. Example: I was driving when I saw him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'while' is used before a longer action, not a short one like 'called'.`,2:`'during' is followed by a noun, not by a clause.`,3:`'as long as' means 'if', which does not fit here.`},options:[`while`,`when`,`during`,`as long as`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-023`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`___ you need help, call me.`,explanation:`Use 'if' for a possible situation. Example: If you are hungry, eat something.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Unless' means 'if not', so the meaning would be wrong.`,2:`'Because' gives a reason, but this sentence is about a possible situation.`,3:`'Although' shows contrast, which does not fit here.`},options:[`Unless`,`If`,`Because`,`Although`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-024`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Paper ___ from wood.`,explanation:`Use the present passive for facts about how things are produced. Example: Cheese is made from milk.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Paper does not make anything; you need the passive.`,2:`The passive needs 'is' before 'made'.`,3:`This is active; paper is not doing the action.`},options:[`is made`,`makes`,`made`,`is making`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-025`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ smoke in the hospital. It's not allowed.`,explanation:`Use 'mustn't' when something is not allowed. Example: You mustn't park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary, not that it is forbidden.`,2:`'might not' shows uncertainty, not a rule.`,3:`'needn't' means it is not necessary, not that it is forbidden.`},options:[`don't have to`,`mustn't`,`might not`,`needn't`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-026`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`There ___ be a cinema here, but now it's a supermarket.`,explanation:`Use 'there used to be' for something that existed in the past but not now. Example: There used to be a park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,1:`'was used' is the passive of 'use' and does not fit before 'be'.`,3:`'uses to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`use to`,`was used`,`used to`,`uses to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-027`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`___ you finished the report yet?`,explanation:`Present perfect questions: Have/Has + subject + past participle. Example: Have you eaten yet?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'has' is used with he/she/it, not with 'you'.`,2:`'are' does not form the present perfect; you need 'have'.`,3:`'do' is followed by the base form, not by 'finished'.`},options:[`Have`,`Has`,`Are`,`Do`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-028`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`She hurt her hand while she ___ the vegetables.`,explanation:`After 'while', use the past continuous for the longer background action. Example: He fell while he was running.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous does not fit with the past 'hurt'.`,1:`The present perfect does not describe the background action in the past.`,2:`The present simple does not fit in a story about the past.`},options:[`is cutting`,`has cut`,`cuts`,`was cutting`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-029`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`You won't pass the exam ___ you study harder.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not'. Example: I won't go unless you come with me.`,wrongReasons:{1:`With 'if', the sentence means studying harder makes you fail.`,2:`'when' means it will surely happen, which gives the wrong meaning.`,3:`'so' shows a result, not a condition.`},options:[`unless`,`if`,`when`,`so`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-030`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The letters ___ every morning at nine.`,explanation:`Present passive for regular actions: am/is/are + past participle. Example: The rooms are cleaned every day.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Letters do not deliver; someone delivers them, so use the passive.`,2:`'letters' is plural, so you need 'are'.`,3:`This is not a full verb; you need 'are delivered'.`},options:[`are delivered`,`deliver`,`is delivered`,`delivering`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-031`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Tomorrow is Sunday, so I ___ get up early.`,explanation:`Use 'don't have to' when something is not necessary. Example: You don't have to pay; it's free.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't' means it is forbidden, but getting up early on Sunday is not against any rule.`,1:`'can't' means it is impossible, which is not the point here.`,3:`'have to' means it is necessary, but Sunday is a free day.`},options:[`mustn't`,`can't`,`don't have to`,`have to`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-032`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`I ___ like vegetables, but now I love them.`,explanation:`Negative: didn't use to + base verb. Example: I didn't use to drink tea.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'didn't', write 'use to' without -d.`,2:`A negative needs 'didn't': 'didn't use to'.`,3:`'used to' is only past, so 'don't use to' is wrong.`},options:[`didn't use to`,`didn't used to`,`not used to`,`don't use to`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-033`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`The train has ___ left. You've missed it.`,explanation:`Use 'already' between has/have and the participle for something that happened sooner than expected. Example: I've already paid.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'yet' goes at the end of negative sentences and questions.`,2:`'ever' is used in questions about experience, not here.`,3:`'since' needs a point in time after it.`},options:[`yet`,`already`,`ever`,`since`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-034`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`At midnight, most people in the village ___.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a specific past time. Example: At ten, I was watching TV.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous does not fit a story about the past.`,2:`The present perfect does not show an action in progress at a past time.`,3:`The present simple talks about habits, not one moment in the past.`},options:[`are sleeping`,`were sleeping`,`have slept`,`sleep`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-035`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Plants die if they ___ enough water.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for general truths: present simple in both parts. Example: Ice melts if it gets warm.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In a zero conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple, not 'will'.`,2:`The past simple does not fit a general fact with 'die'.`,3:`This is not a full verb; you need 'don't get'.`},options:[`won't get`,`don't get`,`didn't get`,`not getting`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-036`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The Mona Lisa ___ by Leonardo da Vinci.`,explanation:`Past passive with 'by' for the person who did it. Example: The song was written by my uncle.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The painting did not paint; you need the passive 'was painted'.`,2:`The painting was made in the past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active; the painting did not paint anything.`},options:[`painted`,`was painted`,`is painted`,`has painted`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-037`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`When I was five, I ___ swim.`,explanation:`Use 'could' for ability in the past. Example: She could read when she was four.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can' is present; 'When I was five' is past.`,2:`'must' shows obligation, not past ability.`,3:`'should' gives advice, not past ability.`},options:[`can`,`could`,`must`,`should`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-038`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`She ___ walk to school, but now she takes the bus.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for a past habit compared with the present. Example: We used to cycle, but now we drive.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not show a finished past habit.`,2:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,3:`'was use to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`used to`,`uses`,`use to`,`was use to`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-039`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`How long ___ in this flat? – Since March.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'how long' for a situation that started in the past and continues now. Example: How long have you had your car?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple cannot be used with 'how long' for a time up to now.`,1:`The present continuous cannot show the time from March until now.`,2:`The past simple is for a finished time; 'since March' goes up to now.`},options:[`do you live`,`are you living`,`did you live`,`have you lived`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-040`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`I saw an accident while I ___ to work this morning.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the background action. Example: I met Ana while I was waiting for the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect means the drive was finished before you saw it.`,1:`The present continuous does not fit with 'saw' and 'this morning'.`,2:`The present perfect is not used for a background action in a past story.`},options:[`had driven`,`am driving`,`have driven`,`was driving`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-041`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If it ___ sunny on Saturday, we'll have a picnic.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: If it is cold, I'll wear a coat.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'if' in a first conditional, use the present simple, not 'will'.`,1:`'would' is not used after 'if' in a first conditional.`,2:`The past simple does not fit with 'we'll have'.`},options:[`will be`,`would be`,`was`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-042`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The windows ___ once a month.`,explanation:`Present passive for routines: are + past participle. Example: The floors are washed every week.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Windows do not clean themselves; use the passive.`,1:`This is active continuous; windows are not doing the action.`,3:`Without 'are', this is not a correct present passive.`},options:[`clean`,`are cleaning`,`are cleaned`,`cleaned`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-043`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`___ I open the window? It's very hot.`,explanation:`Use 'Can I...?' to ask for permission. Example: Can I use your pen?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Am' is not followed by a base verb like 'open'.`,2:`'Have' is not followed by a base verb like 'open' in a question.`,3:`'Does' is used with he/she/it, not with 'I'.`},options:[`Am`,`Can`,`Have`,`Does`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-044`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Where ___ to live before you moved here?`,explanation:`Questions: (Wh-) + did + subject + use to + verb? Example: What did you use to eat for breakfast?`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'did', write 'use' without -d.`,1:`Questions with 'used to' need 'did'.`,3:`'were' does not form questions with 'use to'.`},options:[`did you used`,`used you`,`did you use`,`were you use`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-045`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`I have never ___ to Japan, but I'd love to go.`,explanation:`To talk about visiting a place, use 'have been to'. Example: She has never been to Africa.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'being' is the -ing form; after 'have' you need the past participle.`,2:`'went' is the past simple, not the past participle.`,3:`'go' is the base form; the present perfect needs a participle.`},options:[`been`,`being`,`went`,`go`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-046`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`When the teacher came in, the students ___ quiet immediately.`,explanation:`For one action quickly after another, use the past simple for both. Example: When the bell rang, everyone left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'immediately' shows a quick action, so the continuous does not fit.`,1:`'become' is the base form or present, not past.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit in a past story with 'came'.`},options:[`were becoming`,`become`,`became`,`have become`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-047`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`What will you do if you ___ the job?`,explanation:`First conditional questions: What will you do if + present simple? Example: What will you do if it rains?`,wrongReasons:{0:`Don't use 'will' in the if-clause of a first conditional.`,2:`The past simple does not match 'What will you do'.`,3:`A negative present verb needs 'don't'.`},options:[`won't get`,`don't get`,`didn't get`,`not get`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-048`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`My car ___ yesterday, so I took the bus.`,explanation:`Past passive: was + past participle when we don't know who did it. Example: My bike was stolen last week.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The car did not steal anything; you need the passive.`,2:`'yesterday' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'yesterday'.`},options:[`was stolen`,`stole`,`is stolen`,`has stolen`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-049`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Students ___ use their phones during the exam. It's against the rules.`,explanation:`Use 'mustn't' for something against the rules. Example: You mustn't run by the pool.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary, not that it is forbidden.`,1:`'should' gives advice to do it, but it's against the rules.`,3:`'can' means it is allowed, but the rules say it isn't.`},options:[`don't have to`,`should`,`mustn't`,`can`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-050`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`We ___ go camping every summer when we were kids.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for repeated actions in the past. Example: They used to visit us every Sunday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'are used to' is present and means 'are familiar with'.`,1:`The present simple does not fit with 'when we were kids'.`,3:`'were used' is the passive of 'use' and does not fit here.`},options:[`are used to`,`use`,`used to`,`were used`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-051`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`She has worked at the hospital ___ she finished university.`,explanation:`Use 'since' + a point in time or a past event. Example: I've been happy since I moved here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for' is followed by a length of time, not by a clause with a past event.`,1:`'ago' comes after a length of time and is used with the past simple.`,2:`'from' is not used alone to join two clauses like this.`},options:[`for`,`ago`,`from`,`since`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-052`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`___ it snowing when you left the house?`,explanation:`Past continuous question: was/were + subject + -ing. Example: Was it raining when you arrived?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'did' is followed by the base form, not 'snowing'.`,1:`'is' is present, but 'left' is past.`,2:`'were' is used with you/we/they, not with 'it'.`},options:[`Did`,`Is`,`Were`,`Was`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-053`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If you mix blue and yellow, you ___ green.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: if + present simple, present simple. Example: If you mix red and white, you get pink.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not fit a general fact.`,1:`'getting' is not a full verb here.`,2:`The past perfect does not fit a general fact.`},options:[`got`,`getting`,`had got`,`get`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-054`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Football ___ all over the world.`,explanation:`Present passive for general facts: is + past participle. Example: Tea is drunk in many countries.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Football does not play; people play it, so use the passive.`,2:`This is active continuous; football is not doing the action.`,3:`Without 'is', this is not a correct present passive.`},options:[`plays`,`is played`,`is playing`,`played`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-055`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`She isn't answering her phone. She ___ be busy.`,explanation:`Use 'might' to say something is possibly true. Example: He might be at home.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has' is not followed by a base verb like 'be'; you would need 'has to'.`,1:`'mustn't' means something is forbidden, not a guess.`,2:`'shouldn't' gives advice, not a guess about a situation.`},options:[`has`,`mustn't`,`shouldn't`,`might`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-056`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Our town ___ have so many cars. It was much quieter.`,explanation:`Negative past habits or states: didn't use to + verb. Example: My street didn't use to be so busy.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'used to' is only past; there is no 'doesn't use to'.`,2:`'wasn't' cannot form the negative of 'used to'.`,3:`After 'didn't', the verb must be 'use' without -d.`},options:[`doesn't use to`,`didn't use to`,`wasn't use to`,`didn't used to`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-057`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`This is the best pizza I ___ ever eaten.`,explanation:`After a superlative, use the present perfect with 'ever'. Example: It's the funniest show I've ever seen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'did' is not used with a past participle like 'eaten'.`,2:`'am' does not form the present perfect.`,3:`'has' is used with he/she/it, not with 'I'.`},options:[`did`,`have`,`am`,`has`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-058`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`We weren't watching TV; we ___ a board game.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe what was in progress at a past moment. Example: She wasn't working; she was resting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not fit with 'weren't watching'.`,2:`The present perfect does not describe what was in progress at that moment.`,3:`The present continuous does not match the past 'weren't'.`},options:[`play`,`were playing`,`have played`,`are playing`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-059`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`I'll lend you my laptop if you ___ careful with it.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + verb, if + present simple. Example: I'll help you if you are free.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Don't use 'will' after 'if' in a first conditional.`,1:`'were' is for imaginary situations, but this is a real offer.`,2:`'be' is the base form; you need 'are' with 'you'.`},options:[`will be`,`were`,`be`,`are`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-060`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The film ___ by millions of people last year.`,explanation:`Past passive: was/were + past participle (+ by). Example: The game was seen by many fans.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The film did not watch anything; use the passive 'was watched'.`,2:`'last year' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'last year'.`},options:[`was watched`,`watched`,`is watched`,`has watched`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-061`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Before the accident, Sam ___ run very fast.`,explanation:`Use 'could' for an ability someone had in the past. Example: My grandmother could sing beautifully.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can' is present; 'Before the accident' is past.`,1:`'should' gives advice, not past ability.`,2:`'has to' shows present obligation, not past ability.`},options:[`can`,`should`,`has to`,`could`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-062`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`People ___ write letters much more before email.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' for things that were common in the past. Example: People used to listen to the radio more.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'were used to' means 'were familiar with' and is followed by -ing.`,1:`In positive sentences, write 'used to', with -d.`,2:`'are using to' is not a correct form.`},options:[`were used to`,`use to`,`are using to`,`used to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-063`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`I ___ my grandparents since Christmas.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' for a period up to now. Example: I haven't eaten since breakfast.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The past simple cannot be used with 'since' for a time up to now.`,2:`The present simple does not show a period from Christmas until now.`,3:`The past continuous is for an action in progress in the past.`},options:[`haven't seen`,`didn't see`,`don't see`,`wasn't seeing`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-064`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`The lights went out while the doctor ___ the patient.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for an action in progress when something else happened. Example: The phone rang while I was cooking.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not fit with the past 'went out'.`,2:`The present perfect does not show an action in progress in the past.`,3:`The present continuous is for now, not for a past story.`},options:[`was examining`,`examines`,`has examined`,`is examining`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-065`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If my phone battery dies, I ___ call you.`,explanation:`A modal like 'can' can be used in the result of a first conditional. Example: If it rains, we can stay inside.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'couldn't' is past or imaginary, but this is a real future situation.`,2:`'can' has no 'will' form; say 'won't be able to'.`,3:`The negative of 'can' is 'can't' or 'cannot'.`},options:[`couldn't`,`can't`,`won't can`,`not can`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-066`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Our homework ___ by the teacher every Friday.`,explanation:`Present passive + by for who does it. Example: The bills are paid by my father.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Homework does not check anything; use the passive.`,2:`Without 'is', this is not a correct present passive.`,3:`This is active continuous; the homework is not doing the action.`},options:[`checks`,`is checked`,`checked`,`is checking`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-067`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`In my job, I ___ wear a uniform every day.`,explanation:`Use 'have to' (I/you/we/they) and 'has to' (he/she/it) for obligation. Example: We have to start at eight.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has to' is for he/she/it, not 'I'.`,1:`'must' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`,2:`'having to' is not a full verb here.`},options:[`has to`,`must to`,`having to`,`have to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-068`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`My sister ___ be very shy, but now she talks to everyone.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + be for past states that changed. Example: He used to be thin.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without 'did', the form is 'used to', with -d.`,1:`'is used to' is present and means 'is familiar with'.`,2:`'was used' is passive and does not fit before 'be'.`},options:[`use to`,`is used to`,`was used`,`used to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-069`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`They moved to Canada two years ___.`,explanation:`Use 'ago' with the past simple for a finished time. Example: I started school ten years ago.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'since' needs a point in time, and goes with the present perfect.`,1:`'for' would need a different verb, like 'They lived there for two years'.`,3:`'already' does not follow a length of time like 'two years'.`},options:[`since`,`for`,`ago`,`already`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-070`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`He ___ his phone in the taxi and never saw it again.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for single, finished actions in a story. Example: She lost her bag and called the police.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Leaving the phone is a single, short action, so the continuous does not fit.`,1:`The present perfect does not go with 'never saw' in a finished story.`,3:`The present simple does not fit with the past 'saw'.`},options:[`was leaving`,`has left`,`left`,`leaves`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-071`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If the shop is closed, we ___ to the market instead.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: If the bus is late, I will walk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not talk about a future result.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'is closed'.`,3:`'would go' is for imaginary situations with a past verb after 'if'.`},options:[`have gone`,`went`,`will go`,`would go`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-072`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`These cakes ___ by my grandmother this morning.`,explanation:`Past passive with plural subjects: were + past participle. Example: The photos were taken by Sam.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The cakes did not make anything; use the passive.`,2:`'this morning' is a finished past time here, so use the past passive.`,3:`'cakes' is plural, so you need 'were'.`},options:[`made`,`were made`,`are made`,`was made`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-073`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ see the new science museum. It's amazing!`,explanation:`Use 'should' to recommend something. Example: You should try this cake.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't' means it is not allowed, but the speaker loves the museum.`,2:`'don't have to' means it isn't necessary, which is not a recommendation.`,3:`'shouldn't' advises against it, but the museum is 'amazing'.`},options:[`mustn't`,`should`,`don't have to`,`shouldn't`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-074`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`___ your grandparents use to live on a farm?`,explanation:`Questions with 'used to' start with 'Did'. Example: Did you use to play tennis?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Were' does not form questions with 'use to'.`,1:`'Have' does not form questions with 'use to'.`,2:`'used to' is about the past, so you need 'Did', not 'Do'.`},options:[`Were`,`Have`,`Do`,`Did`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-075`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Have you ___ been to a classical music concert?`,explanation:`Use 'ever' in questions about experience in your life. Example: Have you ever flown in a plane?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'yet' goes at the end of the question, not before the participle.`,2:`'already' asks if something happened sooner than expected, not about life experience.`,3:`'since' needs a point in time after it.`},options:[`yet`,`ever`,`already`,`since`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-076`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`When I arrived at the party, my friends ___. Everyone was on the dance floor.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe a scene already in progress. Example: When I got home, my sister was singing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'danced' would mean they started dancing after you arrived.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,3:`The present simple describes habits, not a past scene.`},options:[`danced`,`were dancing`,`have danced`,`dance`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-077`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If you press this button, the machine ___.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for how things work: present simple in both parts. Example: If you turn this key, the door opens.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not fit with 'press'.`,1:`'starting' is not a full verb here.`,3:`'would' is for imaginary situations, not for how a machine works.`},options:[`started`,`starting`,`starts`,`would start`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-078`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Two people ___ in the accident yesterday.`,explanation:`Past passive: were + past participle; 'hurt' stays 'hurt'. Example: Nobody was hurt in the fire.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'yesterday' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,2:`'two people' is plural, so you need 'were'.`,3:`'hurt' is irregular; the past participle is 'hurt', not 'hurted'.`},options:[`were hurt`,`are hurt`,`was hurt`,`were hurted`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-079`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`___ you help me carry this box, please?`,explanation:`Use 'Could you...?' for polite requests. Example: Could you close the door, please?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Should' asks for advice, not for help.`,1:`'Must' talks about obligation, not a polite request.`,2:`'Might' is not used to ask someone to do something.`},options:[`Should`,`Must`,`Might`,`Could`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-080`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`I used to ___ football every weekend.`,explanation:`After 'used to', always use the base form of the verb. Example: She used to dance.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'used to', use the base form, not -ing.`,2:`After 'used to', use the base form, not the past simple.`,3:`After 'used to', use the base form without -s.`},options:[`playing`,`play`,`played`,`plays`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-081`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`My parents ___ married for thirty years, and they are still happy together.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect + for for a state that started in the past and continues. Example: We have been friends for years.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple cannot be used with 'for thirty years' up to now.`,2:`The past simple means the marriage ended, but they are still together.`,3:`'has' is for he/she/it; 'my parents' is plural.`},options:[`have been`,`are`,`were`,`has been`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-082`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`The sun was shining and the birds ___ in the trees.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to set the scene in a story. Example: It was raining and people were running.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match 'was shining'.`,2:`The present continuous is for now, not a past scene.`,3:`The present perfect does not describe a background scene in the past.`},options:[`sing`,`were singing`,`are singing`,`have sung`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-083`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Call me ___ you get home.`,explanation:`Use 'when' + present simple for something you are sure will happen. Example: Text me when you arrive.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`,2:`'while' is for a longer action, not a moment like arriving home.`,3:`'during' is followed by a noun, not a clause.`},options:[`when`,`unless`,`while`,`during`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-084`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`When ___ the castle built?`,explanation:`Past passive questions: When/Where + was/were + subject + past participle? Example: When was this house built?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'did' is used for active questions; the passive needs 'was'.`,2:`The castle was built in the past, so 'is' is wrong.`,3:`'has' does not form a past passive question.`},options:[`was`,`did`,`is`,`has`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-085`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`I'm not sure, but we ___ go to the beach this weekend.`,explanation:`Use 'might' when you are not sure about a plan. Example: I might visit my aunt on Sunday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must' shows strong obligation, but the speaker is 'not sure'.`,1:`'have to' shows necessity, not uncertainty.`,2:`'mustn't' means it is forbidden, not uncertain.`},options:[`must`,`have to`,`mustn't`,`might`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-086`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Before smartphones, we used to ___ paper maps when we travelled.`,explanation:`Used to + base form for past habits. Example: We used to buy CDs.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'used to', the verb is in the base form, not -ing.`,2:`After 'used to', the verb is in the base form, not the past.`,3:`After 'used to', the verb does not take -s.`},options:[`use`,`using`,`used`,`uses`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-087`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`We ___ to the new museum yet.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'yet' in negative sentences. Example: I haven't been to the new café yet.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not go with 'yet'.`,2:`'aren't' is the present of 'be' and does not talk about visits up to now.`,3:`'wasn't' is singular and the past continuous does not go with 'yet'.`},options:[`haven't been`,`don't go`,`aren't`,`wasn't going`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-088`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`I didn't hear you because I ___ to music.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an activity in progress at a past moment. Example: I missed your call because I was driving.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not explain what was in progress at that moment.`,1:`The present continuous does not match the past 'didn't hear'.`,3:`The present simple does not fit with the past 'didn't hear'.`},options:[`have listened`,`am listening`,`was listening`,`listen`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-089`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If people don't drink enough water, they ___ tired.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: if + present simple, present simple for things that are always true. Example: If you don't sleep, you feel tired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not fit a general truth.`,1:`'would' is for imaginary situations, not general facts.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit a general truth.`},options:[`got`,`would get`,`get`,`had got`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-090`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Credit cards ___ in this shop.`,explanation:`Present passive with a plural subject: are + past participle. Example: Dogs are not allowed here.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Credit cards do not accept anything; use the passive.`,2:`This is active continuous; the cards are not doing the action.`,3:`'credit cards' is plural, so you need 'are'.`},options:[`are accepted`,`accept`,`are accepting`,`is accepted`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-091`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Did you ___ work late yesterday?`,explanation:`For past obligation use 'had to'; in questions: Did + subject + have to? Example: Did she have to wait long?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must' has no past form and cannot follow 'did'.`,1:`After 'did', use the base form 'have to', not 'had to'.`,3:`'should' cannot follow 'did'.`},options:[`must`,`had to`,`have to`,`should`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-092`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`He ___ to be a teacher before he became a writer.`,explanation:`Use 'used to be' for a past job or state. Example: She used to be a nurse.`,wrongReasons:{1:`In positive sentences, write 'used', with -d.`,2:`'used to' has no present form like 'uses to'.`,3:`'was used to be' is not correct; say 'used to be'.`},options:[`used`,`use`,`uses`,`was used`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-093`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`It's the first time I ___ a horse.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect after 'It's the first time'. Example: It's the first time I've tried skiing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'It's the first time', the present simple is wrong.`,2:`After 'It's the first time', use the present perfect, not the past simple.`,3:`After 'It's the first time', the present continuous is wrong.`},options:[`ride`,`have ridden`,`rode`,`am riding`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-094`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`My dad was reading the newspaper while my mum ___ the garden.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for two long actions at the same time. Example: I was studying while he was cooking.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match 'was reading'.`,1:`The present perfect does not show two actions happening at the same time in the past.`,2:`The present continuous is for now, not for the past.`},options:[`waters`,`has watered`,`is watering`,`was watering`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-095`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If I ___ time this evening, I'll call my grandmother.`,explanation:`In the first conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple. Example: If I have money, I'll buy it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Don't use 'will' in the if-clause.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'I'll call'.`,2:`'would' is not used after 'if' in a first conditional.`},options:[`will have`,`had`,`would have`,`have`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-096`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The new school ___ by the mayor last week.`,explanation:`Past passive + by: was + past participle. Example: The hospital was opened by the queen.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The school did not open anything; with 'by the mayor' you need the passive.`,2:`'last week' is past, so the present passive is wrong.`,3:`This is active and the present perfect does not go with 'last week'.`},options:[`was opened`,`opened`,`is opened`,`has opened`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-097`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ bring food to the party. We have plenty.`,explanation:`Use 'don't have to' when something is not necessary. Example: You don't have to come if you're tired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'have to' means it is necessary, but they already have plenty.`,1:`'must' shows strong obligation, which does not fit 'We have plenty'.`,2:`'should' advises bringing food, but it isn't needed.`},options:[`have to`,`must`,`should`,`don't have to`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-098`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Did there use to ___ a bakery on this corner?`,explanation:`Question form: Did there use to be...? Example: Did there use to be a lake here?`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'use to', use the base form, not -ing.`,2:`After 'use to', use the base form, not the past participle.`,3:`After 'use to', use the base form 'be', not 'is'.`},options:[`be`,`being`,`been`,`is`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-099`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Lucy has just ___ an email to her manager.`,explanation:`Use has/have + just + past participle for a very recent action. Example: He has just left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'send' is the base form; after 'has just' you need the past participle.`,1:`'sending' is the -ing form and does not follow 'has'.`,2:`'sends' is the present simple form, not a participle.`},options:[`send`,`sending`,`sends`,`sent`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-100`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Where were you going when I ___ you yesterday?`,explanation:`Use the past simple for the short action. Example: What were you doing when I called?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'see' is not usually used in the continuous form for a short moment.`,1:`The present simple does not fit with 'yesterday'.`,3:`The present perfect is not used with 'yesterday'.`},options:[`was seeing`,`see`,`saw`,`have seen`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-101`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If you don't hurry, you ___ late for school.`,explanation:`First conditional for warnings: if + present simple, will + verb. Example: If you touch that, you'll get hurt.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would be' is for imaginary situations, not a real warning.`,1:`The past simple does not show a future result.`,3:`The present perfect does not show a future result.`},options:[`would be`,`were`,`will be`,`have been`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-102`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The tickets ___ online, not at the door.`,explanation:`Present passive: are + past participle. Example: Newspapers are sold at the station.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'tickets' is plural, so you need 'are'.`,2:`This is active; the tickets are not doing the selling.`,3:`This is not a full verb; you need 'are sold'.`},options:[`are sold`,`is sold`,`are selling`,`selling`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-103`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Children ___ play with matches. It's dangerous.`,explanation:`Use 'shouldn't' to advise against something. Example: You shouldn't eat so much sugar.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'should' advises them to do it, but it's dangerous.`,2:`'don't have to' only means it isn't necessary; it doesn't warn about danger.`,3:`'might' shows possibility, not advice or a warning.`},options:[`shouldn't`,`should`,`don't have to`,`might`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-104`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`My mum didn't ___ drive, but she got her licence last year.`,explanation:`Negative: didn't + use to + base form. Example: He didn't use to like dogs.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'didn't', write 'use to' without -d.`,2:`'using to' is not a correct form.`,3:`'uses to' is not a correct form after 'didn't'.`},options:[`used to`,`use to`,`using to`,`uses to`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-105`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`How many countries ___ visited so far?`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'so far' for a total up to now. Example: How many books have you read so far?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so far' means up to now, so the past simple does not fit.`,1:`In a question, 'have' comes before the subject.`,3:`'are' does not form the present perfect.`},options:[`did you`,`you have`,`have you`,`are you`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-106`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Kate ___ her bike when she fell off.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the longer action and the past simple for the short one. Example: He was cooking when he burned his finger.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous does not fit with the past 'fell'.`,1:`The present perfect does not describe an action in progress in the past.`,2:`The present simple talks about habits, not a past moment.`},options:[`is riding`,`has ridden`,`rides`,`was riding`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-107`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`The alarm rings if someone ___ the door.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: present simple in both clauses. Example: The light comes on if you clap.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In a zero conditional, use the present simple after 'if'.`,1:`The past simple does not fit with 'rings'.`,3:`The present continuous does not fit a general rule like this.`},options:[`will open`,`opened`,`opens`,`is opening`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-108`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`We ___ use our phones in class last year, but now it's allowed.`,explanation:`Use 'couldn't' for something that was not allowed in the past. Example: We couldn't go out after ten.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can't' is present, but 'last year' is past.`,1:`'mustn't' has no past form and does not go with 'last year'.`,2:`'don't have to' is present and means it isn't necessary.`},options:[`can't`,`mustn't`,`don't have to`,`couldn't`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-109`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`We used to live in Spain, but we ___ there now.`,explanation:`Compare a past habit (used to) with the present simple for now. Example: I used to smoke, but I don't smoke now.`,wrongReasons:{0:`This says it wasn't a past habit, but they did live there.`,2:`A present negative needs 'don't'.`,3:`'doesn't' is for he/she/it, not 'we'.`},options:[`didn't use to live`,`don't live`,`not live`,`doesn't live`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-110`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`She enjoys ___ in the sea early in the morning.`,explanation:`'enjoy' is followed by the -ing form: I enjoy reading.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'enjoy' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,2:`After 'enjoy' we cannot use the base form.`,3:`'swam' is the past simple; after 'enjoy' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`to swim`,`swimming`,`swim`,`swam`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-111`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`We decided ___ the train instead of driving.`,explanation:`'decide' is followed by 'to' + base verb: They decided to leave early.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'decide' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,1:`After 'decide' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'took' is a past form; after 'decide' use 'to' + base verb.`},options:[`taking`,`take`,`to take`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-112`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`My brother has given up ___ fizzy drinks.`,explanation:`'give up' (= stop doing) is followed by the -ing form: She gave up eating meat.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'give up' is followed by the -ing form, not the infinitive.`,1:`After a phrasal verb like 'give up' we cannot use the base form.`,2:`'drunk' is the past participle; 'give up' needs the -ing form.`},options:[`to drink`,`drink`,`drunk`,`drinking`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-113`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`The museum guide offered ___ us around the old castle.`,explanation:`'offer' is followed by 'to' + base verb: He offered to help me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'offer' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'offer' we must use 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'shown' is the past participle and does not follow 'offer'.`},options:[`showing`,`to show`,`show`,`shown`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-114`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Would you mind ___ the window? It's cold in here.`,explanation:`'Would you mind' is followed by the -ing form: Would you mind waiting?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'mind' is followed by the -ing form, never by 'to' + verb.`,2:`After 'Would you mind' the base form is not possible.`,3:`'closed' is a past form; polite requests with 'mind' use the -ing form.`},options:[`closing`,`to close`,`close`,`closed`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-115`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`I'm really looking forward to ___ my cousins in Canada.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by -ing: I look forward to meeting you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so the base form cannot follow it.`,1:`'to' is already in the phrase; 'to to see' is wrong, and the verb must be -ing.`,3:`'saw' is the past simple and cannot follow the preposition 'to'.`},options:[`see`,`to see`,`seeing`,`saw`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-116`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`The scientists hope ___ the results next spring.`,explanation:`'hope' is followed by 'to' + base verb: We hope to see you soon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'hope' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'hope' we need 'to' before the base verb.`,3:`'published' is a past form and cannot follow 'hope' here.`},options:[`publishing`,`to publish`,`publish`,`published`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-117`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`He's very good at ___ old bicycles.`,explanation:`After a preposition (at, in, of, about) use the -ing form: She is good at drawing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a preposition like 'at' we cannot use 'to' + verb.`,2:`A preposition is never followed by the base form.`,3:`'repaired' is a past form; after 'at' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`to repair`,`repairing`,`repair`,`repaired`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-118`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Please remember ___ the lights off when you leave the office.`,explanation:`'remember to do' means not forget a future action: Remember to lock the door.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'remember + -ing' is for memories of the past, not for something you must do later.`,2:`After 'remember' the base form without 'to' is not correct.`,3:`'turned' is the past simple and does not fit after 'remember'.`},options:[`to turn`,`turning`,`turn`,`turned`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-119`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`The lawyer suggested ___ the contract again before signing it.`,explanation:`'suggest' is followed by the -ing form: He suggested going by bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'suggest' is not followed by 'to' + verb.`,1:`'suggest' is not followed by the base form alone.`,3:`'to reading' is not a correct pattern after 'suggest'.`},options:[`to read`,`read`,`reading`,`to reading`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-120`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Our neighbours can't afford ___ a new car this year.`,explanation:`'afford' is followed by 'to' + base verb: I can't afford to travel this summer.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'afford' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'afford' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'bought' is a past form; 'afford' needs 'to' + base verb.`},options:[`to buy`,`buying`,`buy`,`bought`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-121`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`I avoid ___ the motorway on Friday evenings because of the traffic.`,explanation:`'avoid' is followed by the -ing form: Try to avoid eating late at night.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'avoid' is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'avoid'.`,2:`'used' is the past simple; after 'avoid' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`to use`,`use`,`used`,`using`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-122`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Tom promised ___ late again.`,explanation:`'promise' takes 'to' + verb; the negative is 'not to' + verb: She promised not to tell anyone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'promise' is followed by 'to' + verb, so the negative is 'not to be', not 'not being'.`,1:`'don't be' is an imperative and cannot follow 'promise'.`,3:`The infinitive after 'promise' needs 'to': 'not to be'.`},options:[`not being`,`don't be`,`not to be`,`not be`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-123`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`___ vegetables in the garden is my grandmother's favourite hobby.`,explanation:`The -ing form can be the subject of a sentence: Swimming is good for you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The base form cannot be the subject of a sentence.`,2:`'Grew' is the past simple and cannot be the subject.`,3:`'Grows' is a verb with -s, not a noun; the subject needs the -ing form.`},options:[`Grow`,`Growing`,`Grew`,`Grows`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-124`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`The children kept ___ questions during the film.`,explanation:`'keep' (= continue doing) is followed by the -ing form: He kept talking.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'keep' (= continue) is followed by the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,1:`After 'keep' we cannot use the base form.`,2:`'asked' is a past form; 'keep' needs the -ing form.`},options:[`to ask`,`ask`,`asked`,`asking`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-125`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Visitors are not allowed ___ photos inside the gallery.`,explanation:`'be allowed' is followed by 'to' + base verb: You are not allowed to park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'be allowed' is followed by 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,1:`After 'be allowed' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'took' is a past form and cannot follow 'allowed'.`},options:[`taking`,`take`,`to take`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-126`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Thank you for ___ me with my homework.`,explanation:`'Thank you for' is followed by the -ing form because 'for' is a preposition: Thank you for coming.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After the preposition 'for' we cannot use 'to' + verb.`,2:`A preposition like 'for' is not followed by the base form.`,3:`'helped' is a past form; after 'for' we need the -ing form.`},options:[`helping`,`to help`,`help`,`helped`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-127`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`The engineers managed ___ the bridge before winter.`,explanation:`'manage' (= succeed in doing something difficult) is followed by 'to' + verb: She managed to open the jar.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'manage' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'manage' we need 'to' before the base verb.`,3:`'finished' is a past form; 'manage' needs 'to' + verb.`},options:[`to finish`,`finishing`,`finish`,`finished`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-128`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Look at those dark clouds! It ___ rain.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for predictions based on what we can see now: Look! The bus is going to leave.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'going to' needs the verb 'be' before it: 'is going to'.`,2:`'It is rain' is wrong; 'is' alone cannot make the future with a verb.`,3:`'will be rain' is wrong; 'will' is followed by the base verb 'rain'.`},options:[`going to`,`is going to`,`is`,`will be`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-129`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I've got the tickets! We ___ the band in concert on Saturday night.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for fixed future arrangements: I'm meeting Anna on Friday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for personal arrangements in the future.`,1:`'saw' is past; Saturday night is in the future.`,3:`The present perfect talks about the past up to now, not a future plan.`},options:[`see`,`saw`,`are seeing`,`have seen`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-130`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`A: The phone is ringing. B: I ___ it.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for decisions made at the moment of speaking: I'm thirsty. I'll get some water.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used for a decision made at the moment of speaking.`,2:`'answered' is past, but B has not answered yet.`,3:`'have answered' says the action is already done, which is not true.`},options:[`'ll answer`,`answer`,`answered`,`have answered`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-131`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`According to the timetable, the train to Manchester ___ at 6.15 tomorrow morning.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for timetables and schedules: The film starts at eight.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'leave' without -s does not agree with 'the train'.`,2:`'left' is past, but the time is tomorrow morning.`,3:`'has left' is the present perfect and cannot be used with 'tomorrow'.`},options:[`leaves`,`leave`,`left`,`has left`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-132`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I promise I ___ anyone your secret.`,explanation:`Use 'will/won't' for promises: I'll call you tonight. I won't be late.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'I promise' we use 'will' or 'won't' for the future, not the present simple.`,2:`'didn't tell' is past; a promise is about the future.`,3:`'haven't told' is about the past, not a promise for the future.`},options:[`won't tell`,`don't tell`,`didn't tell`,`haven't told`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-133`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`What ___ this weekend? Have you got any plans?`,explanation:`Use the present continuous to ask about future plans and arrangements: What are you doing tonight?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'do you do' asks about habits or jobs, not plans for this weekend.`,1:`'did you do' asks about the past, not the coming weekend.`,3:`'have you done' asks about past experience, not future plans.`},options:[`do you do`,`did you do`,`are you doing`,`have you done`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-134`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`She has saved enough money, and she ___ a new laptop next month.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for plans and intentions: I'm going to learn Spanish next year.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for personal future intentions.`,1:`'bought' is past, but the time is next month.`,2:`'has bought' is the present perfect and does not match 'next month'.`},options:[`buys`,`bought`,`has bought`,`is going to buy`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-135`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I think robots ___ many jobs in factories in the future.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for predictions based on opinion, often after 'I think': I think it will be hot tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not express a prediction after 'I think ... in the future'.`,2:`'did' is past, but the sentence is about the future.`,3:`'have done' looks at the past, not the future.`},options:[`will do`,`do`,`did`,`have done`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-136`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Be careful! You ___ that glass of water!`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' when we can see that something is about to happen: Watch out! You're going to fall.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not warn about something that is about to happen.`,2:`'dropped' is past, but the glass has not fallen yet.`,3:`'have dropped' means it has already happened, so 'Be careful!' makes no sense.`},options:[`drop`,`'re going to drop`,`dropped`,`have dropped`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-137`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Don't worry about the heavy bags. I ___ you carry them.`,explanation:`Use 'will' to offer to do something: That looks heavy. I'll carry it for you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used to make an offer.`,1:`'helped' is past, but the offer is for now or later.`,2:`'have helped' says it is finished, but no help has happened yet.`},options:[`help`,`helped`,`have helped`,`'ll help`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-138`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Our flight ___ at 10 a.m., so we need to be at the airport by eight.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for official timetables: The plane lands at 9.30.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'take off' needs -s with 'our flight' (he/she/it).`,2:`'took off' is past, but they still need to go to the airport.`,3:`'has taken off' says the plane has already left.`},options:[`takes off`,`take off`,`took off`,`has taken off`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-139`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`My sister ___ married in June. She has already booked the hall.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for arrangements that are already planned: We're moving house next week.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is used for timetables, not for personal arrangements like a wedding.`,2:`'got' is past, but June is in the future here.`,3:`'has got married' would mean it already happened; 'has got' does not fit.`},options:[`is getting`,`gets`,`got`,`has got`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-140`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I'm sure you ___ the exam. You've studied so hard.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for predictions, especially after 'I'm sure' or 'I think': I'm sure she'll love the present.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not express a prediction after 'I'm sure'.`,2:`'passed' is past, but the exam has not happened yet.`,3:`The present continuous is for arrangements, not predictions like this one.`},options:[`pass`,`'ll pass`,`passed`,`are passing`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-141`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Wait for me here. I ___ back in five minutes.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for promises and quick statements about the near future: I'll be ready soon.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'being' is not a complete verb and cannot follow 'I' alone.`,2:`'was' is past, but 'in five minutes' is the future.`,3:`'have been' is the present perfect and does not match 'in five minutes'.`},options:[`'ll be`,`being`,`was`,`have been`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-142`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`It was announced today: the government ___ a new law about plastic bags next year.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for decided plans: The city is going to build a new bridge.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for a plan like this; it is for timetables and habits.`,1:`'introduced' is past, but the time is next year.`,3:`'has introduced' talks about the past, not next year.`},options:[`introduces`,`introduced`,`is going to introduce`,`has introduced`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-143`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`A: We've run out of milk. B: Oh, really? I ___ some on my way home.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for a decision made at the moment of speaking: It's cold. I'll close the window.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for a decision you make while speaking.`,1:`'bought' is past; B has just learned about the milk.`,3:`'have bought' says it is already done, which is not true.`},options:[`buy`,`bought`,`'ll buy`,`have bought`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-144`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`___ I carry your suitcase for you? It looks heavy.`,explanation:`Use 'Shall I...?' to offer help: Shall I open the door for you?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Do I carry...?' asks about a habit, not an offer.`,1:`'Am I carry' is wrong; 'am' cannot go with the base form 'carry'.`,3:`'Have I carry' is wrong; 'have' needs a past participle, and it is not an offer.`},options:[`Do`,`Am`,`Shall`,`Have`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-145`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Next year my parents ___ their thirtieth wedding anniversary.`,explanation:`Use 'will' (or 'be going to') to talk about future events: Next month I will be twenty.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'celebrated' is past, but the time is next year.`,1:`'have celebrated' is about the past, not next year.`,3:`'celebrating' needs 'are' before it to make a verb.`},options:[`celebrated`,`have celebrated`,`will celebrate`,`celebrating`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-146`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The woman ___ lives next door is a nurse.`,explanation:`Use 'who' (or 'that') for people: The man who called you is my uncle.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' is for things and animals, not people.`,2:`'where' is for places, not people.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun.`},options:[`which`,`who`,`where`,`whose`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-147`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This is the hotel ___ we stayed last summer.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places: That's the shop where I bought my bike.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' would need 'in' at the end ('which we stayed in').`,2:`'who' is for people, not places.`,3:`'what' is not used to start a relative clause after a noun.`},options:[`which`,`where`,`who`,`what`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-148`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`I lost the umbrella ___ my aunt gave me.`,explanation:`Use 'that' or 'which' for things: The book that you lent me is great.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'where' is for places, not objects like an umbrella.`,1:`'who' is for people, not things.`,2:`'what' cannot follow a noun to start a relative clause.`},options:[`where`,`who`,`what`,`that`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-149`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The scientist ___ discovered this medicine won an important prize.`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people: The girl who sings in the choir is my sister.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' is used for things, but a scientist is a person.`,2:`'where' refers to a place.`,3:`'what' does not follow a noun in a relative clause.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`where`,`what`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-150`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Do you know the name of the town ___ the famous painter was born?`,explanation:`Use 'where' after a noun for a place: This is the village where I grew up.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' would need 'in' after 'born' ('which he was born in').`,1:`'who' is for people, but the gap refers to the town.`,2:`'when' is for times, not places.`},options:[`which`,`who`,`when`,`where`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-151`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The laptop ___ I bought last week has already stopped working.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things: The car which he drives is very old.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who' is for people, not objects.`,2:`'where' is for places; a laptop is a thing.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`where`,`whose`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-152`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Is that the boy ___ dog bit the postman?`,explanation:`Use 'whose' to show possession: That's the woman whose car was stolen.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'who' cannot show possession; we need 'whose dog'.`,2:`'who's' means 'who is', which does not fit before 'dog'.`,3:`'which' is for things and does not show possession here.`},options:[`whose`,`who`,`who's`,`which`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-153`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The film ___ we watched last night was really boring.`,explanation:`Use 'that' or 'which' for things: The song that she sang was beautiful.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'who' is only for people.`,2:`'where' is for places, not films.`,3:`'what' cannot follow a noun like 'the film'.`},options:[`that`,`who`,`where`,`what`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-154`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`A dictionary is a book ___ tells you the meaning of words.`,explanation:`Use 'which' or 'that' for things: A fridge is a machine which keeps food cold.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who' is for people, but a book is a thing.`,1:`'where' is for places.`,2:`'what' is not used after a noun to describe it.`},options:[`who`,`where`,`what`,`which`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-155`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The police officer ___ helped us was very kind.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people in defining relative clauses: The teacher that helped me was great.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' is for things, not people.`,1:`'where' is for places, not people.`,2:`'whose' must be followed by a noun, like 'whose car'.`},options:[`which`,`where`,`whose`,`that`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-156`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`This is the café ___ my parents first met.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for a place: That's the park where we play football.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' would need 'in' or 'at' ('which my parents first met in').`,2:`'who' refers to people, not a café.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and needs a noun after it.`},options:[`where`,`which`,`who`,`whose`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-157`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`My grandfather, ___ is 85, still goes swimming every day.`,explanation:`In a clause between commas, use 'who' for people and 'which' for things, never 'that': My aunt, who lives in Paris, is a doctor.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'that' cannot be used in a clause between commas.`,1:`'which' is for things, not people.`,3:`'whose' must be followed by a noun.`},options:[`that`,`which`,`who`,`whose`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-158`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Paris, ___ I visited in 2019, is full of beautiful buildings.`,explanation:`In a clause between commas, use 'which' for things and places when the verb needs an object: Rome, which I love, is very old.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'that' is not used in a clause between commas.`,1:`'where' is wrong because 'visit' needs an object: we visit a place, not visit 'in' it.`,2:`'who' is for people, not cities.`},options:[`that`,`where`,`who`,`which`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-159`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`I have a friend ___ brother plays in a rock band.`,explanation:`Use 'whose' + noun to show possession: I know a girl whose mother is a pilot.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who brother' is wrong; to show possession we need 'whose'.`,2:`'that' cannot show possession.`,3:`'which' is for things and does not show possession.`},options:[`who`,`whose`,`that`,`which`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-160`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The students ___ passed the test will get a certificate.`,explanation:`Use 'who' or 'that' for people: The players who won got a medal.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'which' is for things, not students.`,2:`'whose' needs a noun after it, like 'whose names'.`,3:`'where' refers to a place, not people.`},options:[`who`,`which`,`whose`,`where`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-161`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`The park ___ we used to play as children is now a car park.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places when there is no preposition at the end: This is the room where I sleep.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'which' would need 'in' at the end ('which we used to play in').`,1:`'who' is for people, not a park.`,2:`'that' would need 'in' at the end ('that we used to play in').`},options:[`which`,`who`,`that`,`where`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-162`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`What's the name of the app ___ helps you learn languages?`,explanation:`Use 'that' or 'which' for things: I have an app that counts my steps.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'who' is for people, not apps.`,1:`'where' is for places.`,3:`'whose' shows possession and must have a noun after it.`},options:[`who`,`where`,`that`,`whose`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-163`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Can you ___ my cat while I'm on holiday?`,explanation:`'look after' means take care of someone or something: She looks after her little brother.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'look for' means try to find something.`,2:`'look up' means find information, for example in a dictionary.`,3:`'look into' means investigate a problem.`},options:[`look for`,`look after`,`look up`,`look into`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-164`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`I don't know this word. I'll look it ___ in the dictionary.`,explanation:`'look something up' means find information in a book or online: Look it up on the internet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'look after' means take care of someone.`,1:`'look for' means try to find something you have lost.`,3:`'look out' means be careful.`},options:[`after`,`for`,`up`,`out`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-165`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`It's hot in here. You can ___ your jacket if you like.`,explanation:`'take off' means remove clothes: Take off your shoes, please.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put on' means start wearing, which is the opposite of what you want when it's hot.`,1:`'take up' means start a new hobby.`,3:`'turn off' is for machines and lights, not clothes.`},options:[`put on`,`take up`,`take off`,`turn off`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-166`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`The plane ___ late because of the fog.`,explanation:`'take off' (for planes) means leave the ground: The plane took off at noon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'took up' means started a hobby.`,1:`'took after' means looked or behaved like a parent.`,2:`'took over' means took control of something.`},options:[`took up`,`took after`,`took over`,`took off`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-167`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`She ___ her mother: they both have curly red hair.`,explanation:`'take after' means look or behave like an older family member: He takes after his father.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'takes off' means removes or leaves the ground.`,2:`'looks after' means takes care of someone.`,3:`'brings up' means raises a child.`},options:[`takes after`,`takes off`,`looks after`,`brings up`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-168`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`We've ___ sugar, so I can't make a cake.`,explanation:`'run out of' means have no more of something: We've run out of bread.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'run into' means meet someone by chance.`,1:`'run away from' means escape from a place or person.`,3:`'run over' means hit someone or something with a car.`},options:[`run into`,`run away from`,`run out of`,`run over`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-169`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Can you ___ the music? I'm trying to sleep.`,explanation:`'turn down' means make a sound quieter: Please turn down the TV.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'turn up' means make it louder, the opposite of what you need.`,2:`'turn on' means start a machine.`,3:`'turn into' means change into something different.`},options:[`turn up`,`turn down`,`turn on`,`turn into`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-170`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`The meeting was ___ because the manager was ill.`,explanation:`'call off' means cancel: They called off the match because of the snow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'called back' means phoned someone again.`,2:`'put on' means organised an event, which does not match an ill manager.`,3:`'taken off' is for clothes or planes, not meetings.`},options:[`called back`,`called off`,`put on`,`taken off`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-171`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`I ___ an old school friend at the station yesterday. What a surprise!`,explanation:`'run into' means meet someone by chance: I ran into my teacher at the market.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'ran out' means had no more of something.`,1:`'ran after' means chased someone.`,3:`'ran over' means hit someone with a car.`},options:[`ran out`,`ran after`,`ran into`,`ran over`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-172`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`My grandparents ___ me up because my parents worked abroad.`,explanation:`'bring up' means look after a child until it is an adult: She was brought up by her aunt.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'grow up' cannot have an object: you grow up, but nobody 'grows you up'.`,1:`'look up' means find information, not raise a child.`,3:`'take up' means start a hobby.`},options:[`grew`,`looked`,`brought`,`took`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-173`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`I'm going to ___ smoking next month. It's bad for my health.`,explanation:`'give up' means stop doing something: He gave up coffee last year.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'give in' means stop fighting or arguing and accept something.`,2:`'give back' means return something to its owner.`,3:`'give away' means give something free to someone.`},options:[`give up`,`give in`,`give back`,`give away`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-174`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`The firefighters quickly ___ the fire.`,explanation:`'put out' means stop a fire burning: Put out the candle before you go to bed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'put off' means delay something until later.`,1:`'put on' means start wearing or switch on.`,2:`'put up' means build or fix something on a wall.`},options:[`put off`,`put on`,`put up`,`put out`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-175`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`We had to ___ the football match until next week because of the rain.`,explanation:`'put off' means move something to a later time: Don't put off your homework.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'put out' means stop a fire.`,2:`'put up' means build or hang something.`,3:`'put down' means place something on a surface.`},options:[`put off`,`put out`,`put up`,`put down`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-176`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`I usually ___ at 6.30 on weekdays, but I stay in bed later at the weekend.`,explanation:`'get up' means leave your bed in the morning: I get up early every day.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'get on' means enter a bus or train, or have a good relationship.`,1:`'get off' means leave a bus or train.`,3:`'get over' means recover from an illness or a problem.`},options:[`get on`,`get off`,`get up`,`get over`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-177`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`It took him weeks to ___ his cold.`,explanation:`'get over' means recover from an illness or a bad experience: She got over the flu quickly.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'get up' means leave your bed.`,1:`'get off' means leave a bus or train.`,2:`'get on' means enter a bus or have a good relationship.`},options:[`get up`,`get off`,`get on`,`get over`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-178`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Can you ___ the children from school at 3 o'clock? I have a meeting.`,explanation:`'pick up' means collect someone, often by car: I'll pick you up at the station.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'pick out' means choose one thing from a group.`,1:`'take up' means start a hobby.`,2:`'pick on' means treat someone badly.`},options:[`pick out`,`take up`,`pick on`,`pick up`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-179`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Our car ___ on the way to the airport, so we took a taxi.`,explanation:`'break down' means stop working (for machines and cars): The bus broke down in the rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'broke up' means ended a relationship.`,1:`'broke out' means started suddenly, like a war or a fire.`,3:`'broke in' means entered a building illegally.`},options:[`broke up`,`broke out`,`broke down`,`broke in`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-180`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`___! There's a car coming!`,explanation:`'Look out!' means be careful because of danger: Look out! The floor is wet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Look up' means search for information or raise your eyes.`,1:`'Look after' means take care of someone.`,2:`'Look into' means investigate.`},options:[`Look up`,`Look after`,`Look into`,`Look out`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-181`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`I stayed at home ___ I had a bad headache.`,explanation:`'because' introduces a reason: I was late because I missed the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so' introduces a result, but the headache is the reason.`,1:`'although' shows a contrast, but there is no contrast here.`,3:`'however' is used at the start of a new sentence, not to join two clauses like this.`},options:[`so`,`although`,`because`,`however`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-182`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`It was raining, ___ we took our umbrellas.`,explanation:`'so' introduces a result: I was hungry, so I made a sandwich.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'because' introduces a reason, but taking umbrellas is a result.`,2:`'although' shows contrast, but this is cause and result.`,3:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`so`,`because`,`although`,`unless`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-183`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`___ he trained hard, he didn't win the race.`,explanation:`'although' shows contrast between two ideas: Although it was late, we kept working.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Because' gives a reason, but training hard is not a reason for losing.`,2:`'So' introduces a result and cannot start this kind of clause.`,3:`'However' cannot join two clauses in one sentence like this.`},options:[`Although`,`Because`,`So`,`However`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-184`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`The hotel was expensive. ___, the staff were very friendly.`,explanation:`'However' introduces a contrast at the start of a new sentence, followed by a comma: It was cold. However, we went out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because' cannot start a sentence followed by a comma like this; it needs a clause.`,2:`'So' shows a result, but friendly staff is not a result of high prices.`,3:`'Although' must join two clauses in the same sentence.`},options:[`Because`,`However`,`So`,`Although`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-185`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`I'd like to come to the party, ___ I have to work that evening.`,explanation:`'but' joins two contrasting ideas: I like tennis, but I don't play it often.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so' shows a result, but working is not a result of wanting to come.`,1:`'because' gives a reason, but working is not why you want to come.`,2:`'or' gives a choice, which does not fit here.`},options:[`so`,`because`,`or`,`but`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-186`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`She went to bed early ___ she was very tired.`,explanation:`'because' gives the reason for something: He smiled because he was happy.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'although' shows contrast, but being tired is a reason to go to bed.`,2:`'so' introduces a result, but being tired is the cause here.`,3:`'but' shows contrast, which does not fit this cause.`},options:[`although`,`because`,`so`,`but`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-187`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`We missed the bus, ___ we were late for the lesson.`,explanation:`'so' introduces the result of something: It was sunny, so we went to the beach.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'because' gives a reason, but being late is the result here.`,1:`'although' shows contrast, but this is cause and result.`,2:`'unless' means 'if not', which does not fit.`},options:[`because`,`although`,`unless`,`so`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-188`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`___ the weather was bad, the festival continued.`,explanation:`'Although' + clause shows contrast: Although she was ill, she went to work.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because of' is followed by a noun, and it gives a reason, not a contrast.`,2:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or -ing, not a full clause like 'the weather was bad'.`,3:`'However' cannot join two clauses in one sentence like this.`},options:[`Because of`,`Although`,`Despite`,`However`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-189`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`My phone wasn't working. ___, I couldn't call you.`,explanation:`'As a result' introduces a result at the start of a new sentence: The road was closed. As a result, we were late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Although' shows contrast and must join two clauses in one sentence.`,2:`'Because' gives a reason and cannot be followed by a comma like this.`,3:`'Unless' means 'if not' and does not fit a result.`},options:[`Although`,`As a result`,`Because`,`Unless`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-190`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`You can't drive a car ___ you have a licence.`,explanation:`'unless' means 'if not': You can't enter unless you have a ticket.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'if' makes the sentence mean you can't drive when you have a licence, which is wrong.`,2:`'because' gives a reason, which makes the meaning strange.`,3:`'so' introduces a result, which does not fit here.`},options:[`if`,`unless`,`because`,`so`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-191`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`I like coffee, ___ my wife prefers tea.`,explanation:`'but' shows a contrast between two ideas: He is tall, but his brother is short.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so' shows a result, but my wife's taste is not a result of mine.`,1:`'because' gives a reason, which does not fit here.`,2:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`so`,`because`,`unless`,`but`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-192`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`We stayed inside ___ the storm.`,explanation:`'because of' + noun gives a reason: The match was cancelled because of the rain.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'because' must be followed by a clause with a verb, not just a noun.`,2:`'although' needs a clause and shows contrast, not a reason.`,3:`'so' introduces a result and needs a clause.`},options:[`because of`,`because`,`although`,`so`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-193`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`___ I was walking home, I saw an accident.`,explanation:`'while' + clause means 'at the same time as': While I was cooking, the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'During' is followed by a noun, not a clause with a verb.`,2:`'Because of' is followed by a noun and gives a reason.`,3:`'Despite' shows contrast and is followed by a noun or -ing.`},options:[`During`,`While`,`Because of`,`Despite`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-194`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`We went swimming in the sea ___ the water was very cold.`,explanation:`'even though' is a strong way to show contrast: She went out even though it was raining.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'because' gives a reason, but cold water is not a reason to swim.`,2:`'so that' shows a purpose, which does not fit here.`,3:`'unless' means 'if not', which makes no sense here.`},options:[`even though`,`because`,`so that`,`unless`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-195`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Turn off the lights when you leave ___ save energy.`,explanation:`Use 'to' + base verb to show purpose: I went to the shop to buy milk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for' is followed by a noun or -ing, not the base verb.`,2:`'so' needs a clause with a subject, like 'so we save energy'.`,3:`'because' needs a full clause with a subject.`},options:[`for`,`to`,`so`,`because`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-196`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`I've never been to Japan. ___, I'd love to go one day.`,explanation:`'However' shows contrast with the sentence before: I'm tired. However, I'll finish the work.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Because' gives a reason and cannot be followed by a comma at the start like this.`,1:`'So that' shows purpose and needs to join two clauses.`,2:`'Unless' means 'if not', which does not fit here.`},options:[`Because`,`So that`,`Unless`,`However`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-197`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`First, mix the flour and the eggs. Then add the milk. ___, put the mixture in the oven.`,explanation:`'Finally' introduces the last step in a list of instructions: Finally, serve the soup hot.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Although' shows contrast and needs two clauses.`,2:`'Because' gives a reason, not the last step.`,3:`'However' shows contrast, not the order of steps.`},options:[`Finally`,`Although`,`Because`,`However`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-198`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Could you ___ me your pen for a minute?`,explanation:`'lend' means give something to someone for a short time: Can you lend me your bike?`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'borrow' something from someone; you don't 'borrow me' something.`,1:`'rent' is for paying money to use something, like a flat.`,2:`'take me your pen' is wrong; 'take' does not mean give.`},options:[`borrow`,`rent`,`take`,`lend`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-199`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`I need to ___ some money from the bank to buy a house.`,explanation:`'borrow' means take something that you will give back later: I borrowed a book from the library.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'lend' is what the bank does; you take money, so you borrow.`,2:`'spend' means use money to buy things, not get it from a bank.`,3:`'pay' means give money to someone, not receive it.`},options:[`borrow`,`lend`,`spend`,`pay`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-200`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`The doctor gave me a ___ for some medicine.`,explanation:`A 'prescription' is a doctor's note for medicine: Take this prescription to the chemist's.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A 'recipe' tells you how to cook something.`,1:`A 'receipt' is the paper you get after you pay.`,3:`A 'ticket' lets you travel or enter an event.`},options:[`recipe`,`receipt`,`prescription`,`ticket`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-201`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`We ___ the bus by two minutes and had to wait half an hour for the next one.`,explanation:`'miss' a bus, train or plane means arrive too late to catch it: Hurry or we'll miss the train.`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'lose' things like keys; for transport you didn't catch, use 'miss'.`,1:`'fail' is for exams or tests, not buses.`,2:`'passed' would mean the bus went by you, not that you arrived too late.`},options:[`lost`,`failed`,`passed`,`missed`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-202`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`She works very hard and ___ a good salary.`,explanation:`'earn' means get money for work: He earns 2,000 euros a month.`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'win' a prize or a game, not a salary.`,1:`'gain' is used for weight or experience, not a salary.`,2:`'reach' means arrive at a place or level, not get money for work.`},options:[`wins`,`gains`,`reaches`,`earns`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-203`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Can you ___ me to buy some bread on the way home?`,explanation:`'remind someone to do something' means help them not forget: Remind me to call Mum.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'remember' is not followed by 'me to'; you remember something yourself.`,2:`'recall' means remember something from the past.`,3:`'forget' is the opposite of what you want.`},options:[`remember`,`remind`,`recall`,`forget`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-204`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`The ___ for the concert were sold out in an hour.`,explanation:`A 'ticket' lets you enter an event or travel: I bought two tickets for the match.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'receipts' are papers you get after paying.`,2:`'notes' are short written messages or paper money.`,3:`'bills' tell you how much you need to pay.`},options:[`receipts`,`tickets`,`notes`,`bills`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-205`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Our new flat is very ___: the shops and the station are only five minutes away.`,explanation:`'convenient' means easy and useful, often because it is near: The hotel is convenient for the airport.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'comfortable' describes how nice it feels inside, not the distance to shops.`,1:`'careful' describes people who avoid danger, not places.`,3:`'lonely' describes a sad feeling of being alone.`},options:[`comfortable`,`careful`,`convenient`,`lonely`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-206`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`I'm ___ in history, so I often watch documentaries about ancient Rome.`,explanation:`Use '-ed' adjectives for how people feel: I'm interested in music. The film was interesting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'interesting' describes the thing, not how a person feels.`,2:`'bored' means you don't like it, which doesn't match watching documentaries.`,3:`'boring' describes a thing and has the opposite meaning.`},options:[`interesting`,`interested`,`bored`,`boring`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-207`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Please ___ the dishes after dinner.`,explanation:`We say 'do the dishes' (= wash them): I'll do the dishes tonight.`,wrongReasons:{0:`We 'make' a meal or a cake, but we 'do' the dishes.`,1:`'take the dishes' means carry them, not wash them.`,3:`'have the dishes' does not mean wash them.`},options:[`make`,`take`,`do`,`have`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-208`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`I'm going to ___ a cake for my brother's birthday.`,explanation:`We 'make' food such as cakes and sandwiches: She made a pizza for dinner.`,wrongReasons:{0:`We 'do' jobs and homework, but we 'make' food like cakes.`,2:`'cook a cake' is not natural; we say 'make' or 'bake' a cake.`,3:`'take a cake' means carry it, not prepare it.`},options:[`do`,`make`,`cook`,`take`],correct:1},{id:`b1-mc-209`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`The weather ___ says it will be sunny tomorrow.`,explanation:`A 'weather forecast' says what the weather will be like: Check the forecast before you go.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'prediction' is general; for weather we say 'forecast'.`,2:`A 'guess' is an answer without information, not a weather report.`,3:`A 'view' is what you can see or an opinion.`},options:[`forecast`,`prediction`,`guess`,`view`],correct:0},{id:`b1-mc-210`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`He ___ his keys in the park and never found them again.`,explanation:`'lose' means not be able to find something: I lost my wallet yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'missed' is for buses, trains or people you didn't see, not keys.`,1:`'fail' is for exams and tests.`,2:`'waste' means use badly, like time or money.`},options:[`missed`,`failed`,`wasted`,`lost`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-211`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Excuse me, how much does this jacket ___?`,explanation:`Use 'cost' to talk about the price of something: How much does it cost?`,wrongReasons:{0:`People 'pay' for things; a jacket does not pay.`,1:`People 'spend' money; a jacket does not spend.`,2:`'price' is a noun here, not a verb.`},options:[`pay`,`spend`,`price`,`cost`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-212`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`I'd like to ___ a table for four people at 8 o'clock, please.`,explanation:`'book' means arrange to have something in the future: I booked a room for two nights.`,wrongReasons:{0:`You 'order' food, not a table.`,1:`'keep a table' means not give it away; it is not used for restaurants.`,3:`'rent' means pay to use something for a long time, like a flat.`},options:[`order`,`keep`,`book`,`rent`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-213`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`The ___ at the museum explained the history of every painting.`,explanation:`A 'guide' shows visitors a place and gives information: Our guide was very funny.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A 'driver' drives a car or bus.`,1:`A 'pilot' flies planes.`,2:`A 'waiter' serves food in a restaurant.`},options:[`driver`,`pilot`,`waiter`,`guide`],correct:3},{id:`b1-mc-214`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`This soup has no ___. Can you pass me the salt?`,explanation:`'taste' is the flavour of food: This cake has a lovely taste.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A 'view' is what you see from a window; salt doesn't change it.`,1:`'noise' is a sound, which soup doesn't have.`,3:`Salt doesn't change the 'colour' of soup.`},options:[`view`,`noise`,`taste`,`colour`],correct:2},{id:`b1-mc-215`,type:`multiple-choice`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`It's a long ___ from London to Edinburgh by train.`,explanation:`A 'journey' is one trip from one place to another: The journey takes four hours.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'travel' is uncountable, so we can't say 'a long travel'.`,2:`A 'flight' is by plane, not by train.`,3:`A 'voyage' is a long trip by sea.`},options:[`travel`,`journey`,`flight`,`voyage`],correct:1}]};export{e as default};